 "text_script": "A star about 30 million light-years away keeps erupting like a supernova, and surviving. At least 27 bursts between 2012 and 2026. They call it a supernova impostor. Why? Chandra caught it at least 100 times brighter in X-rays than before its outbursts began. The best explanation: a hidden black hole or neutron star, pulling material off it every 113 days. It is the first X-ray detection of this class of erupting star. Next: Voyager 1 reaches one light-day from Earth, Wednesday, November 18. Follow Downlink Cat for that milestone."
